WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:02.060
- [Presenter] Could commercial planes

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:04.570
soon be powered by clean energy?

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:05.800
The aviation industry,

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.870
which accounts for around
5% of all greenhouse gases

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:11.640
has been experimenting with
technologies like battery,

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.770
solar and even hydrogen.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.600
So far none of these projects
have gone beyond testing,

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.837
and are far from being
commercially viable.

00:00:18.837 --> 00:00:21.080
But in the midst of an
industry-wide crisis

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.010
caused by coronavirus pandemic,

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.170
the world's largest planemaker, Airbus,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.770
announced plans to put
zero emission aircraft

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.520
into commercial service by 2035.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.410
- We unveil today three concept planes.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.940
- [Presenter] So, why now?

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.690
Well economists, climate change
experts and engineers alike

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.590
are arguing that if governments
attach climate conditions

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:44.770
to bailouts handed out in the pandemic,

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.300
it could not only help
save the aviation industry,

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.160
but it could also help
reduce its carbon footprint,

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.580
and crucially create new jobs.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.510
- It's in these periods
of recession and downturn

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.300
that governments spend the most.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.700
And so really it is the
single greatest opportunity

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.050
to redirect the future of our industries.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.380
- [Presenter] So, could
the coronavirus pandemic

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.143
lead to green air travel?

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:12.660
The aviation industry has
been hit hard by the pandemic.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.070
A 95% drop in air travel in April

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.950
has led to airlines canceling
orders for new planes

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:21.360
and Boeing and Airbus axing
tens of thousands of workers

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.930
with governments stepping
in to provide aid.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:27.440
In the US $25 billion has
been offered to US airlines

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:28.980
through the Cares Act.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.760
But some in the industry as
well as scholars and officials

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:32.660
believe that governments

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.470
should be attaching climate
conditions to stimulus.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:38.340
- There's an opportunity to
combine economic performance

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.750
and for positive
environmental performance.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.700
The benefits of adding
green conditionality

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.230
to these bailouts is that you
both protect the current jobs

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:51.880
and you induce new jobs
both in the short-term

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.200
in some of the research
intensive industries,

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.180
but then also in the long-term

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.240
in terms of actually
building new products.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.120
- [Presenter] So, what
options are available

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.600
for the sector to decarbonize?

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.810
Well, the industry has
been exploring ideas

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.420
for a few decades.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.530
Despite its continued heavy dependency

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.610
on kerosene-based jet fuel,

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:15.060
it has managed to reduce
its emissions per passenger

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.270
by some 70% since the 1960s.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:21.040
Simply by focusing on making
planes that are lighter

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.490
and can fly further using less fuel.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.400
The problem is there are
way more people flying now

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.960
than there were in the sixties,

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.630
and that's wiped out all of those gains.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.890
And while researchers predict
the pandemic will lead

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.720
to emissions dropping by
more than a third this year,

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.780
it's expected that air
traffic will rebound

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.100
in the coming years.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.670
All of which leaves the
sector nowhere closer

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.840
to hitting targets of
cutting CO2 emissions in half

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.340
by 2050, compared to 2005 levels.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.670
- If we want to make another big change

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.430
in emissions per passenger,

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.780
we need to radically change things.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:57.760
- [Presenter] Gregoire Carpentier

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.550
is co-founder of Supaero Decarbo,
a group of former students

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:03.790
from France's leading aviation university,

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:06.390
who are lobbying for the
industry to decarbonize.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:07.820
He believes that in its ambitions

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.780
to become less carbon intensive,

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.090
the aviation industry faces a problem

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.960
unlike many other industries.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:18.960
- Therein massive amount of
energy in a minimum space,

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:23.560
otherwise you cannot carry
that energy in the air.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.390
- [Presenter] While many
alternatives to jet fuel

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:28.770
have been proposed they all
share one common problem,

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:33.770
right now nothing is as efficient
or economical as kerosene.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:38.430
- Kerosene as been the
most concentrated energy

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.170
that we ever found.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:43.030
- [Presenter] Biofuels
derived from organic biomass

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.290
are an alternative
that's already available,

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.570
but it's currently more
expensive than kerosene

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:49.250
and requires huge investment

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:51.370
to achieve widespread availability.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:54.120
Battery technology has been
tested by some companies,

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.310
including Airbus,

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:56.980
but the company believes
they're just too heavy

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:58.950
to be economically viable in planes.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.400
- We don't believe that really

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.730
it's a today relevant technology

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.890
for large commercial aircraft.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.770
- [Presenter] And solar
simply doesn't generate

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:07.850
enough power.

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.910
This solar-powered plane made
by start-up Solar Impulse

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.170
has a wingspan wider than a 747

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.310
and is as light as a family car,

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:18.460
but can still only
average 47 miles per hour.

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:20.520
This is where Airbus is hoping hydrogen

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.037
can become aviation's fuel of the future.

00:04:23.037 --> 00:04:26.110
- One particular technology pathway,

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:29.230
which we find most promising is hydrogen.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:30.960
- [Presenter] Remember
those plane designs?

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.230
Airbus has said it can
have these hydrogen planes

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.340
in production by 2035

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.470
and is hoping that by announcing them now,

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.100
it can prompt the rest of
the industry to follow suit.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:41.480
- We're putting a stake in the ground

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:45.540
and making it very clear
that the aviation industry

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.680
needs to also be powered
by renewable energy.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.760
- [Presenter] But even
hydrogen has its problems.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:54.090
Right now it's projected to be
more expensive than kerosene

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:55.620
and would take up more space,

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.810
which means less seats on the plane.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:01.250
- It's four times more
volumic than kerosene

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:02.790
so we store it in the fuselage

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.330
again and you see these
aft part of the fuselage

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.070
being windowless.

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:09.920
- [Presenter] There's also
the problem that hydrogen

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.230
is currently synthesized
mostly by burning fossil fuels.

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:16.010
And while it might be good
for powering small vehicles,

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:17.520
it may not be efficient enough

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.270
to power large planes
on long haul flights.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:22.330
- At least in the first iteration

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.030
of hydrogen aircraft technology,

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:29.670
long range will not be a
market segment that we address.

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:32.040
- [Presenter] And of course,
none of this technology

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.420
or its development comes cheap.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.900
When talking about its
ambition to make these planes

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:37.780
Airbus says...

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:41.540
- That is an ambition
which will require billions

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:42.950
in terms of investment.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:44.510
- [Presenter] Airbus hopes
that some of that money

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:45.820
will come from the government,

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:48.980
if it's to turn its hydrogen
concepts into a reality.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.540
And that's where these
bailout funds could come in.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.210
In France Airbus' home,

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:55.240
the government has
attached climate conditions

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:56.820
to recent investments.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:57.840
Air France for example,

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.270
was bailed out by the French government

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:02.790
to the tune of some $8.2
billion in June this year,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:03.830
under the condition

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:07.450
that the airline halves it's
carbon emissions by 2030.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:09.240
But while France may be using the pandemic

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.310
to push for its aviation
industry to reduce emissions,

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:16.080
for some countries that opportunity
may already have passed.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.560
Brian O'callaghan's research

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.920
studied thousands of economic policies

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.470
and found that many have not
opted for climate conditions.

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:27.420
- In total we find that
at least $140 billion

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:28.870
has been spent on aviation.

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:31.630
Now of that we've seen only $14 billion,

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:33.830
only 10% with green conditions.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:36.470
That is 90% of airline bailouts

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:40.520
that's going towards the
continuation of a dirty industry

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:43.443
without enabling the long-term transition.

00:06:43.443 --> 00:06:47.026
(light instrumental music)

